Research Logbook Entry
Hours Worked: 7 hours 
Project: MW–M31 Halo Shape Evolution – Density Movie Development
Date: March 5, 2026


Summary of Work
---------------

Developed a multi-method halo density movie using the MW–M31 
simulation snapshots. The following density estimation techniques 
were implemented and incorporated into a FFMpegWriter animation:

1. 2D Histogram (baseline): counts of particles per spatial bin; 
   fast and memory-efficient (O(N_particles) time, bins^2 memory).

2. Gaussian Kernel Density Estimation (KDE): smooth density 
   field highlighting faint tidal streams; computationally heavier 
   (O(N_particles * bins^2) time, bins^2 memory).

3. SPH-inspired smoothing: per-particle Gaussian kernel 
   accumulation to emphasize local density variations; highest 
   fidelity but computationally intensive (O(N_particles * bins^2)).

Work also included:
- Centering halos using MW center of mass.
- Applying log-scaling to density maps to improve visualization of 
  both dense cores and sparse tidal features.
- Adding detailed commentary in code on physical interpretation, 
  computational complexity, and memory usage.
- Preparing framework for producing separate movies per density method 
  to track different structural features independently.

Outcome:
- Completed a flexible, research-grade density movie pipeline capable 
  of rendering multi-method visualizations of halo density evolution 
  during the MW–M31 merger.
